  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss, so it is steeper along x. Without noise, x overshoots when the learning rate is above 0.4 and diverges above 0.8.",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 1.5·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Each step: position −= learning rate × (true slope + noise). The orange arrow is that next step; the grey arrow is learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 8. Off-bowl positions are drawn at the bowl's edge; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Run plays five steps a second; Step once does one step. New run, keep last path restarts from the same start with the next noise values and keeps the last run as a faded ghost."
  ]
}